{"content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: `/what changed/i` alone is broad enough to false-positive on honest no-change analyses**

A response like "### What changed\nNothing — the fix is already present in the target branch..." matches this branch and costs a full resume round (agent re-invocation + re-run of the post-action) before the attempts budget exhausts. The docblock correctly notes false negatives are safe; false positives burn the most expensive resource in this loop. Cheap hardening: require the section to actually name a changed file (e.g. combine with the change-verb/file-extension regex), or anchor the heading (`/^#{1,4}\s*what changed/mi`). Current behavior is bounded by attempt exhaustion, so this is polish, not a blocker.
